the humanitarian crisis on europe's  eastern flank shows no sign of easing  poland is pushing back multiple groups  of migrants trying to get over the  border from belarus those who make it  across mostly young families from iraq  are left to face harsh winter conditions  out in the open  now local polish residents are rallying  around to help dw's amnesia reports  [Applause]  a person's place is not in the woods  they shout mothers from around poland  have come to the border region near  belarus to show their concern for the  crisis unfolding here at least a dozen  migrants have died in the forest just  kilometers away a number of them on the  polish side of the border  one of the latest victims was a  one-year-old child  this is so close to our home yes it's  only three hours  by the vicarious from from warsaw and  how can i say to my kids that some other  kids are  frozen  in the forest yes and we are not helping  them  i'm from the south so it took me like  seven hours to come here  but this is my first protest i've ever  taken part of so this is something that  i need i felt that i need to speak about  and it needs to be said  some mothers traveled here in a bus  bringing with them supplies and  gratitude for the local volunteers  helping save lives in the woods  one of these volunteers is monica  tworkovska she lives near the border and  local residents can often provide help  where aid agencies are banned  she found a volunteer network online and  joined just a few days ago  in my opinion it's not about just  letting everybody in  we have different agencies to verify all  of these people let them do this  and we as a people we as a country our  volunteer and all of our different  humanitarian organizations should be  allowed to go in and to help  we cannot let those people die in the  woods when they are freezing hungry  thirsty sick  this is not humane  the polish government says that if it  were to welcome the migrants with open  arms this would play into the hands of  belarus's alleged plan to destabilize  the eu  but it's clear that this hardline policy  has had casualties and more tragedy is  sure to come as winter approaches  and our correspondent amy nassif joins  me now from hinovka on the polish side  of the border amin why is it easier for  locals to help the migrants hiding in  the forest than it is for aid  organizations for example  well nick poland has essentially built a  second border around its real border  with belarus  there's checkpoints along the road so  that people can't get in people like  journalists civil society but local  residents can pass through if they live  on the other side or if they have an id  showing that they live in the area then  there's lighter restrictions on them  going into the restricted area and out  of the restricted area  as i said  as a journalist we have not been allowed  into this  restricted zone and that goes for aid  organizations as well like the red cross  and the un uh uh refugee commission  so  local activists are just i wouldn't even  call them activists just local  volunteers people who have seen the  headlines seen that people are dying in  the woods have uh taken up uh the flag  and have  tried to go in there and just provide  any aid they can so as we saw in the  report monica who's never done anything  like this before she's now thrown into a  situation where she's having to deal  with media she's having to deal  with  people claiming asylum going in between  lawyers and border patrol and  migrants who are in the hospital  so she's overwhelmed but she says that  this is something that she couldn't just  let happen on her doorstep  yeah and i think you know  watching those mothers you could hear  the compassion and  anger as well i think in in their voices  uh  clearly moved by the idea of children  having to spend the night  in the forest and occasionally you know  some some of them even freezing to death  do you think  that feeling is going to spread through  polish society are these protests  changing how how the population is  feeling  i think for sure the protests are an  indication that the uh the polish are  seeing this a little bit differently  than they did a few weeks ago and that's  because the situation has changed at  least how it's being reported so in the  beginning of the crisis poland's uh hard  their line was that the humanitarian  crisis was happening only on the  belarusian side and that was more true a  few weeks ago than it is now because uh  lukashenko has ordered that the camps be  evacuated along the border a lot of  those uh migrants who are trying to get  into poland have been brought to  shelters and only trying to cross at  night so what you see now is that this  double border this no ago zone on the  polish border on the polish side of the  border has become an open-air prison for  people who have crossed the fence but  have not yet been picked up by police  and that's where we're seeing a lot of  the recent deaths happening including a  one-year-old child last week so i think  these headlines have started to change  opinion and that's why you saw this  protest this wasn't a protest that's  been long organized by long-established  aid groups and activists this was  something that really brought mothers  from around the country we talked to  people from krakow people from warsaw  and also people from the local area and  i think we can understand the reasons  for their mobilization very dramatic and  tragic pictures you've been sharing with  us thank you eminencif in high norfolk  poland
 

